        "525719f47a474b33a1eb695ce774c53d": {
          "model_module": "@jupyter-widgets/controls",
          "model_module_version": "1.5.0",
          "model_name": "TextareaModel",
          "state": {
            "_dom_classes": [],
            "_model_module": "@jupyter-widgets/controls",
            "_model_module_version": "1.5.0",
            "_model_name": "TextareaModel",
            "_view_count": null,
            "_view_module": "@jupyter-widgets/controls",
            "_view_module_version": "1.5.0",
            "_view_name": "TextareaView",
            "continuous_update": true,
            "description": "Feature Selection Explanation:",
            "description_tooltip": null,
            "disabled": false,
            "layout": "IPY_MODEL_6dc5171347064266acc3df31de6c748a",
            "placeholder": "<student to fill this section>",
            "rows": null,
            "style": "IPY_MODEL_46f6c928f9364b758bbb9c01fb80217b",
            "value": "The features that relevant to personal information such as prefix, first_name, last_name, gender, phone_number and email, address information such as secondary_address, building_number, street_name, street_suffix are removed out of the dataset, since those do not provide valuable insights or meaningful correlation with the target feature for modeling. \n\nThe correlation heatmap between numerical variables and the rent feature, number_of_bedrooms, number_of_bathrooms, and floor_area have moderate correlation with the rent. Therefore, we select them for feature list. \n\nThe scatterplot between advertised_date and the rent shows that the rental prices maybe follow an upward trend over month, therefore, it is selected.\n\nThe scatterplot between suburb and the rent indicates that the rent prices are different in different cities. Sydney has the widest rent range, whil Canberra has the lowest rent range, and other suburbs have the consistent rent prices. Therefore, we select this variable for modeling.\n\nAbout the tenancy_preference feature, Bachelors/Family tend to pay higher rent than Bachelors and Family individually. It suggests that tenant also affect the rent prices.  \n\nThe point_of_contact variable, agents might handle more premium or high-value properties, with the higher rent prices, while Contact Owner listings tend to have the lower to mid rent range. Therefore, it is selected for feature list. \n\nThe furnished variable also impact the target feature. Unfurnished tend to has the lower prices than Semi-furnished and Furnished, although unfurnished and semi-furnished have nearly the same frequency.\n\nThe level feature has rent values distribute across all levels, some levels might be associated with higher rent ranges, suggesting that there could be some relationship between \"level\" and rent. Therefore, it is choosen for training model. "
          }
        },
