            "style": "IPY_MODEL_62e7537c0e1e48589743201d09013eeb",
            "value": "Best validation performance has alpha = 0.1, l1_ratio = 0.7 with RMSE = 47.52, while training set has the same alpha and l1_ratio with RMSE = 92.65\n\nThis model achieves the lowest validation RMSE, indicating a good generalization ability. The train RMSE is higher, which suggests that the model is not overfitting and maintains good bias-variance trade-off.\n\nBest test performance has alpha = 0.1, l1_ratio = 1.0 (pure Lasso) with RMSE = 54.65\n\nAlthough this configuration does not have the lowest validation RMSE, it achieves better performance on the unseen test set, suggesting that pure L1 regularization (Lasso) helped reduce overfitting and increased robustness when making predictions on new data.\n\nNow we compare the line chart of alpha = 0.1 and l1_ratio = 0.7, alpha = 0.1 and l1_ratio = 1.0. Both models indicate the general upward trend of the rental prices. The predicted line for l1_ratio = 1.0 appears slightly smoother, while l1_ratio = 0.7 seems to respond more closely to fluctuations, potentially making it more sensitive to noise, that's why alpha = 0.1 and l1_ratio = 1.0 has the best performance on testing set.\n\nNeither model perfectly follows the ideal diagonal line, but both provide reasonably accurate predictions for most of the mid-range rent values.\n\nThe performance gap between training and validation, and test suggests that regularization successfully prevents overfitting.\n"
